---
title: "Data-type reference"
description: "A reference for the types the pipeline passes around: the block model, the diff ops, and the server's own state."
---

The rest of this book explains *how* the pipeline, the diff, and the executor
work. This chapter is the **catalog of the types they pass around**, so you can
read [the rendering pipeline](rendering.tmd), [the block model & protocol](block-model.tmd),
or [execution](execution.tmd) without keeping `model.rs` and `diff.rs` open in a
second window.

Two crates own the types. The **core** (`crates/core`) defines the render block
model, the things the parser emits: `Cell`, `Block`, `RenderedDoc`, and friends.
The **server** (`crates/server`) defines the wire-protocol diagnostic and the
private execution bookkeeping. Where the two meet is the one shared type the
core hands the server: `Block` (carrying an optional `Cell`), which the server
runs and splices outputs back into.

## The block model (`render/model.rs`)

### `Block`

One top-level element. The parser emits a `Vec<Block>`; everything downstream,
the diff, click-to-source, live-state preservation, keys off these three data
attributes baked into `html`.

```rust
struct Block {
    id: String,                  // content-hash id, "b-<hex>" (+ "-N" for a duplicate)
    sourcepos: String,           // "startLine:startCol-endLine:endCol", relative to source_file
    source_file: Option<String>, // Some(path) only for a block from an {{< include >}}d file
    html: String,                // rendered HTML; the root element carries the three attrs
    cell: Option<Cell>,          // Some(..) when this block is an executable code cell
    nested: Vec<Block>,          // the cells a ::: container folded away, in document order
}
```

`id` is an FNV-1a hash of the block's raw Markdown source, prefixed `b-` (with a
positional `-N` suffix to break ties between byte-identical blocks). Because it is
content-derived, an unchanged block keeps its id across edits, which is the whole
basis for the incremental diff. `cell` is the bridge to the server: a `Some(..)`
marks a block the executor must run.

`nested` is the second half of that bridge, and it exists because a `:::` container
concatenates its children into one `html` string. A cell inside a callout or a tabset has
no block of its own left, so it is invisible to an executor that scans the flat top-level
list, which is exactly what it was until this field arrived: the cell rendered and never
ran. The container keeps the folded child blocks here, and leaves an **empty output slot**
in its own HTML after each one — a `<div class="tali-output">` carrying that cell's
`{id}-out` block id plus a `data-tali-out-for` marker. The executor fills the slot in
place, so a tab's output lands in that tab's own panel rather than below the whole tabset.

Entries are flattened to one level (a container that folds another container takes its
list wholesale), so an entry's own `nested` is always empty. The rule has no exception: a
container block never carries a `Cell` of its own, so every folded cell that runs is
reachable here and nowhere else.

### `Cell`

The executable payload of a code cell (`` ```{python} ``, `` ```{r} ``, `` ```{js} ``).
The core records it; the server runs `{python}`/`{r}` against a kernel and the
client runs `{js}` in the browser.

```rust
struct Cell {
    lang: String,                  // "python" | "r" | "js" | ...
    code: String,                  // the cell body, options stripped
    figure: Option<CellFigure>,    // #| label: fig-x + #| fig-cap: → numbered figure
    table: Option<CellTable>,      // #| label: tbl-x + #| tbl-cap: → numbered table
    echo: bool,                    // #| echo: false hides source (cell still runs, output shows)
    include: bool,                 // #| include: false hides source AND output (still runs)
    cache: bool,                   // #| cache: false → never freeze; always re-run
    js: JsOpts,                    // {js} cell wiring; default/empty for every other language
}
```

`echo`, `include`, and `cache` default to `true` (the front-matter `execute:`
block sets the document default, the `#|` option overrides per cell).

### `JsOpts`

The `//|` options of a native `{js}` cell, the surface that wires it into the
client-side reactive runtime. Empty (`Default`) for every kernel cell.

```rust
struct JsOpts {
    name: Option<String>,   // //| name: x → publish the return value under "x" into the shared scope
    viewof: Option<String>, // //| viewof: x → the cell returns a DOM input registered as "x"
    inputs: Vec<String>,    // //| input: a, b → re-run when input "a" or "b" (or a `define` value) changes
}
```

::: {.callout-note}
The cell option is spelled `//| input:` (singular) in the source; it parses to the
plural `inputs` vector because one cell can depend on several.
:::

### `CellFigure` / `CellTable`

Figure and table metadata folded onto a cell, so the **executor** can wrap the
cell's *output* once it exists. The output HTML isn't known until the cell runs,
which is why this is metadata on the cell rather than a separate block: the
executor reads it, then wraps the executed output in a `<figure>` (with the
`#fig-x` anchor and caption) or injects a numbered `<caption>` into the executed
`<table>`.

```rust
struct CellFigure {
    anchor: Option<String>,   // "#fig-…" when labelled, so @fig-x cross-refs resolve
    caption: Option<String>,
    number: usize,            // assigned in document order
}

struct CellTable {
    anchor: Option<String>,   // "#tbl-…" when labelled, so @tbl-x cross-refs resolve
    caption: Option<String>,
    number: u32,              // assigned in document order alongside Markdown tables
}
```

### `CellRole`

A cell's numbered cross-reference role, derived from its `#| label:` /
`#| *-cap:` options. Unlike `CellFigure` / `CellTable` (which live *on* the
`Cell`), this is `pub(crate)` to the renderer; it is the one place `#| label:
lst-x` and `#| code-fold` land. A `lst-` listing is the standout case: it wraps
the cell's **source**, not its output, and `fold` carries the `code-fold`
start-open flag plus the summary text so a folded listing renders.

```rust
enum CellRole {
    // label: fig-x / fig-cap → a numbered figure (the cell's OUTPUT).
    Figure { anchor: Option<String>, caption: Option<String> },
    // label: lst-x / lst-cap → a numbered listing (the cell's SOURCE);
    // fold = Some((start_open, summary)) when #| code-fold is set.
    Listing { anchor: Option<String>, caption: Option<String>, fold: Option<(bool, String)> },
    // label: tbl-x / tbl-cap → a numbered table (the cell's executed OUTPUT).
    Table { anchor: Option<String>, caption: Option<String> },
}
```

### `DocFormat`

The output format from the front-matter `format:` key. Decides which page
scaffold (and which live client) is emitted.

```rust
enum DocFormat {
    Html,   // default: a standard HTML page (blog post, book page)
    Reveal, // a slide deck (format: deck / *-deck)
}
```

::: {.callout-note}
The enum variant is `Reveal` for historical reasons (the deck engine used to be
reveal.js). The engine is now Taliesin's own; reveal.js was removed. The variant
name is the only fossil left: the runtime exposes `window.TaliesinDeck` and nothing
else, with no back-compat alias and no `window.Reveal`. See
[the deck engine](deck-engine.tmd).
:::

### `OutputMode`

How aggressively a page trims its optional machinery, threaded from the `build`
CLI through the page builders. The live preview always uses `Preview`, so the dev
loop is never affected by build-time gating.

| Variant | When | What ships |
|---|---|---|
| `Preview` | live dev preview (the default) | every enhancer, unconditionally (a doc can gain any construct on the next edit, so gating would race the live mount) |
| `Build` | static `build` (including `--stdout`) | DOM-specific enhancer scripts gated to what the page actually contains; `code-enhance/` (reader menu + a11y) still ships on every page |
| `Bare` | `build --bare` | zero `<script>`, zero CDN, CSS-only theming; for a rough draft, an archive, or a future print pipeline |

### `Warning`

A non-fatal render warning the **core** produces. The core can't return a
`Result` for these (it returns a `RenderedDoc` regardless), so it collects them on
`RenderedDoc::warnings` for the server to log and surface. A located warning (`line`
set) renders as a clickable jump-to-source row in the dev menu.

```rust
struct Warning {
    message: String,
    file: Option<String>, // doc-base-relative (matches Block::source_file); None = the previewed doc
    line: Option<u32>,    // Some(..) makes it click-to-source; None is an unlocated warning
}
```

Build it with `Warning::new(msg)` for an unlocated warning, then `.at(file, line)`
to attach a source location.

### `RenderedDoc`

The output of `render_document_with_includes`: an ordered block list plus the
front-matter metadata the page builders need.

```rust
struct RenderedDoc {
    title: Option<String>,
    subtitle: Option<String>,
    lang: Option<String>,         // front-matter lang: (BCP-47), → <html lang>; None falls back to "en"
    description: Option<String>,  // SEO/OpenGraph meta for a standalone page
    format: DocFormat,
    toc: bool,                    // effective TOC flag (a site recomputes this from its default)
    toc_explicit: Option<bool>,   // the page's explicit toc: as a tri-state, so a site can tell off from unset
    theme_css: String,            // resolved custom theme CSS; empty for the built-in light/dark themes
    theme_default: String,        // "dark" | "light" force it; "auto" follows prefers-color-scheme
    theme_is_custom: bool,        // a custom theme owns the colours (decks skip built-in light/dark management)
    includes: PageIncludes,       // resolved include-* + css front-matter markup
    warnings: Vec<Warning>,
    blocks: Vec<Block>,
}
```

`body_html()` is the one helper: it concatenates the block HTML, one block per line,
the body the `full_render` message and the static `build` both emit.

### `PageIncludes`

The already-resolved markup for the page template's three injection slots. `in_header`
comes from the project's `_site.yml` `head:`, fully resolved (an inline `text:`, or a
referenced file's contents) so the template just drops it in. `before_body` and
`after_body` have no configured source since the raw-injection family was retired on
2026-08-02; `before_body` is still written by the site chrome, which is where the
draft-page banner comes from.

```rust
struct PageIncludes {
    in_header: String,      // → <head>
    before_body: String,    // → just after <body>
    after_body: String,     // → just before </body>
}
```

::: {.callout-warning}
These strings are injected **verbatim and unescaped**. That is by design (the
author is trusted, see the trust model in [the block model & protocol](block-model.tmd#trust-model)),
but it means nothing untrusted may flow into them without sanitizing first.
:::

`merge(other)` appends `other` after `self` (site-level includes first, then the
page's own), slot by slot.

### Includes & the per-line origin map (`includes.rs`)

Include resolution inlines an `{{< include path >}}`'d file *before* parsing, so
the merged buffer has contiguous lines comrak can give exact sourcepos for. To map
those merged lines back to their real file, it records a **`LineOrigin`** for every
line of the expanded text:

```rust
struct LineOrigin {
    file: Option<String>, // origin file; None for the primary doc, Some(path) for an included file
    line: usize,          // its original 1-based line number in that file
}
```

This is what becomes `Block::source_file` and lets click-to-source open the right
file at the right line even for a block that came from an included partial. See the
pipeline's [include-resolution stage](rendering.tmd#stage-by-stage).

### `IncludeWarning`

The other thing the same module returns: a located diagnostic for an
`{{< include >}}` that *couldn't* be expanded (an unsafe path, a cycle, or an
unreadable file). `resolve_warned` hands one back per dropped directive so the
build, the preview, and `check` can surface it as a click-to-source row instead
of silently shipping the literal `{{< include … >}}`.

```rust
struct IncludeWarning {
    target: String,       // the raw include target as written in the directive
    reason: String,       // short human phrase: why it couldn't resolve
    file: Option<String>, // file holding the directive (None = primary doc), matches LineOrigin::file
    line: usize,          // 1-based line of the directive within `file`
}
```

## The diff (`diff.rs`)

### `BlockOp`

`diff_blocks(old, new) -> Vec<BlockOp>` turns two block lists into the minimal set
of DOM mutations the client applies. Ids in `target_id` / `after_id` refer to
elements **currently in the DOM**; the `html` payload carries the block's *new* id.
The mechanism (an LCS over the ids, the `SetMeta` trick, and why removes are sorted
before inserts) is covered in [the block model & protocol](block-model.tmd#the-diff-diff-rs);
here are just the four variants.

```rust
enum BlockOp {
    // Replace the element target_id with html (which carries the new id), then pulse it.
    Update { target_id: String, html: String },
    // Insert html after after_id (or prepend at the start when None).
    Insert { after_id: Option<String>, html: String },
    // Remove the element target_id.
    Remove { target_id: String },
    // Patch ONLY the position metadata of target_id, leaving its rendered content and
    // its live DOM state in place. Emitted when an edit elsewhere shifted an unchanged
    // block's line numbers: same content-hash id and body, only the attribute moved.
    SetMeta { target_id: String, sourcepos: String, source_file: Option<String> },
}
```

`protocol::op` (in the server) serializes each variant to the wire JSON, mapping each
variant to its message `type` string (`update` / `insert` / `remove` / `set_meta`) the
client switches on. See [the websocket protocol](protocol.tmd) for each message's wire shape.

## Server types

### `Diagnostic` (`protocol.rs`)

The server's wire-protocol counterpart to the core's `Warning`: a non-fatal issue
surfaced in the preview, serialized into the `full_render` and `diagnostics`
messages. It adds a `level` (the core's `Warning` is always a warning) and an
optional inline code `frame`.

```rust
struct Diagnostic {
    level: &'static str,    // "warning" | "error"
    message: String,
    file: Option<String>,   // doc-base-relative; None = the previewed doc
    line: Option<u32>,      // 1-based; Some(..) makes the row click-to-source
    frame: Option<String>,  // optional small code frame (a few source lines, the offending one marked)
}
```

Construct it with `Diagnostic::warn(msg)` / `Diagnostic::error(msg)`, then chain
`.at(file, line)` to make it clickable and `.with_frame(frame)` to attach the code
frame.

| | `Warning` (core) | `Diagnostic` (server) |
|---|---|---|
| crate | `taliesin-core`, `render/model.rs` | `taliesin-server`, `protocol.rs` |
| levels | warning only | `"warning"` / `"error"` |
| code frame | no | yes (`frame`) |
| produced by | the render pipeline | the server (held in each doc's state, includes the kernel state and front-matter linter) |

### `CellRef` / `Ran` (`exec.rs`, internal)

These two are **private to the executor** (not part of any public API); they appear
here only so [the execution chapter](execution.tmd) reads cleanly. `CellRef` is a
code cell pulled out of the block list with everything needed to run it and rebuild
its output block (`block_index`, `id`, `code`, `sourcepos`, `source_file`, plus the
same `figure` / `table` / `include` / `cache` it carried on the
`Cell`). `Ran` is one cell the **current warm kernel** has already executed: its
cumulative cache key (`hash`) and the `output` HTML it produced. The ordered list
of `Ran`s doubles as the record of what state the live kernel holds, which the
planner diffs the next run against to find the warm prefix. The cumulative cache
key itself lives in [the freeze cache](execution.tmd#the-persistent-cache-freeze).
